---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="How QuiiCast Works — Screen Share Without an Account"
	description="Learn how to screen share online without installing anything. QuiiCast connects two browsers directly with a 4-digit code — here's how it works."
	path="/how-it-works"
>
	<div class="text-center">
		<h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white sm:text-4xl">
			How to screen share online without installing anything
		</h1>
		<p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
			QuiiCast is a screen sharing tool that runs entirely in your browser. There's nothing to
			install and no account to create — just a 4-digit code that connects two browsers directly.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-14 space-y-5">
		{[
			{ n: '1', title: 'Start a cast', body: 'On the <a href="/caster" class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-500 dark:text-brand-300 dark:hover:text-brand-200">cast page</a>, click "start sharing." Your browser will ask for permission to share your screen, a window, or a tab — this permission prompt is a browser security requirement and can\'t be skipped. Once you allow it, QuiiCast generates a random 4-digit code.' },
			{ n: '2', title: 'Share the code', body: 'Give that code to whoever you want to share your screen with — up to 5 people can join the same cast with it. It expires automatically after a few minutes.' },
			{ n: '3', title: 'Connect as a receiver', body: 'Each person goes to the <a href="/receiver" class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-500 dark:text-brand-300 dark:hover:text-brand-200">receive page</a> and types in the code. As soon as it\'s validated, their browser negotiates its own direct, peer-to-peer video connection with yours — QuiiCast\'s server helps browsers find each other but never sees or touches the actual video stream.' }
		].map((step) => (
			<div class="glass-card flex gap-5 p-6">
				<span class="flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-gradient-to-br from-brand-400 to-brand-600 text-sm font-bold text-white shadow-lg shadow-brand-600/30">
					{step.n}
				</span>
				<div>
					<h2 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">{step.title}</h2>
					<p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400" set:html={step.body} />
				</div>
			</div>
		))}
	</div>

	<div class="glass-card mt-8 p-6">
		<h2 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Screen share on the same WiFi network</h2>
		<p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
			If both devices are on the same WiFi network, turn on "Prefer local WiFi" before starting
			your cast. This keeps the actual video traffic on your local network for lower latency —
			QuiiCast still needs a brief moment of internet access to help the two browsers connect, but
			the video itself never has to leave the network.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-10">
		<h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Good to know</h2>
		<ul class="mt-4 space-y-3">
			{[
				'No sign-up, no download, no browser extension.',
				'Works in any modern desktop or mobile browser to receive a screen share.',
				"iOS Safari can't share (cast) a screen due to Apple's platform restrictions, but it can receive one.",
				'Up to 5 people can watch the same cast at once — built for small groups, not broadcasting to a large audience.'
			].map((item) => (
				<li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
					<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-brand-400">
						<path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5" />
					</svg>
					{item}
				</li>
			))}
		</ul>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
